{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**Chasing Cherry Blossoms in Kyoto**\n\nThe first time I stepped onto the platform in Kyoto, the scent of matcha tea and the distant hum of traditional temple bells filled the air. This trip was about more than sightseeing—it was about losing myself in the rhythms of old Japan.\n\nI started in **Fushimi Inari**, where thousands of vermilion torii gates stretched up the sacred mountain. Each step felt like a pilgrimage, the gates blurring into a surreal tunnel of color. By the time I reached the summit, the sun had risen, casting long shadows over the city below.\n\nAfter a quick bowl of **ramen** (rich, brothy, and perfectly salty), I wandered through **Arashiyama**, where bamboo groves swayed in the breeze. The towering stalks created a near-magical corridor, their green fronds whispering secrets. Nearby, the **Kamo River** mirrored the blooming *sakura*, their soft pink petals drifting like snow.\n\nIn **Gion**, Kyoto’s geisha district, lanterns flickered even in the daytime, casting a golden glow on wooden lanterns and quiet streets. I spotted a few *maiko* (apprentice geisha) hurrying between appointments, their ornate *kimono* a splash of color against the muted tones of the old town.\n\nMy last stop was **Kinkaku-ji**, the Golden Pavilion, its top two floors gleaming under the afternoon sun. As I sipped green tea in the garden, the stillness was broken only by the rustle of cherry leaves—proof that beauty, here, was in the fleeting moments.\n\nJapan didn’t just leave an impression; it rewrote my sense of peace. And as the train pulled away from Kyoto station, I knew I’d be back.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06942010670900345}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -4.231684684753418}, {"token": "asing", "logprob": -0.055498577654361725}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob": -0.4946403503417969}, {"token": " Bl", "logprob": -0.00368368